const SLOW_RAMP := 0.0025      # slow motion ramps LINEARLY in and out per frame
							   # (a hard step made the player miss, Louis; slowed
							   # further so both easing in and out give real
							   # reaction time instead of a sudden gear change)
const SLOW_MIN_BALLS := 3      # under three props the crowd holds no breath:
							   # no chain or Amazing! farming with two balls
# --- lessons (Louis): a lesson is taught in thick time, so a beginner can SEE the arc
# and the window. It starts nearly frozen and speeds up as the lesson goes on, so the
# last step is close to real speed and the player is never dumped from a slow world
# straight into a fast one. A step may override this floor with its own "slow" key.
const TUT_SLOW_START := 0.5    # the first step of a lesson: half speed (the
								# slow-stack cap, so lessons stay jitter-free too)
# THE PACE LADDER, one shape for EVERY lesson (2026-07-27, Louis): every drill in
# every lesson is run three times over - half speed, quarter speed, then full speed.
# The two slow passes are GUIDED, which here means something much stronger than a
# prompt: the world STOPS DEAD at every single action the drill asks for, names the
# key (or the combination, in the order it goes) that unfreezes it, hears nothing
# else, and makes the press land - a guided catch is always caught, a guided throw
# always thrown. So the SHAPE of a pattern is learned with the timing taken away,
# twice, before the last pass at 0% slowmo asks the player to find the beat alone.
# This replaced an adaptive ladder [0.5, 0.25, 0.15, 0.0] that sped up on clean
# catches and slowed on drops: it taught the rhythm by attrition, and a beginner who
# could not find the first beat never saw the second.
const TUT_PHASES: Array[float] = [0.5, 0.25, 0.0]
# THE TWO STYLES (2026-07-27, Louis). Every juggler holds a pattern one of two ways,
# and which one you are doing changes every single beat - so the lessons name them,
# teach them apart, and say what the difference is.
#
#   BEGINNER keeps props IN THE HANDS. With three, two are held and one is up. As it
#   comes down you throw the one in the receiving hand FIRST, then catch: throw, catch,
#   throw, catch. The air is never fuller than it has to be, so there is only ever one
#   thing to track. (This is what the game's own basics lesson teaches.)
#
#   ADVANCED keeps NOTHING in the hands. You put every prop up at the start, at a
#   spacing you judge yourself, and from then on each beat is catch, then throw the
#   same prop straight back: the exact opposite order, on the exact same two keys.
#
# So a beat is "J then K" for a beginner and "K then J" for an advanced juggler, and
# the ORDER is the lesson. The opening is the only place a single key is ever asked
# for on its own: n-2 throws to fill a beginner's air, n to fill an advanced one's.
const TUT_BEGINNER := "beginner"
const TUT_ADVANCED := "advanced"
const TUT_EXCHANGE_LEAD := 14  # ticks before a prop lands that its hand is stopped and
								# asked for its beat. A hand still holding one is asked to
								# THROW first and then catch (the "J then K" exchange the
								# whole cascade is made of); an empty hand is just asked to
								# catch. Small enough that the prop is visibly AT the hand,
								# and far inside a lesson's Perfect window (W_LATE), so the
								# guided press always scores Perfect.
const TUT_HOLD_GAP := 8        # ticks of real motion after a satisfied beat before the
								# next one may stop the world - so the throw and the catch
								# the player just made are actually SEEN happening
const TUT_STEP_READ := 30      # ...and a longer breath at the top of each step, to read it
const PROMPT_LEAD := 90        # ticks before a catch that its key prompt begins to glow
							   # and brighten (at the 50% slow cap, SLOW_TOTAL_CAP, that
							   # is about 1.5 real seconds)
const SPIN_CATCH_MULT := 2.0       # every catch during a Spin scores double
const SPIN_ACC_PENALTY := 0.28     # a Spin lowers the off-Perfect catch chance (juggling blind)

# --- THE ACT (Phase 2, reworked per Louis) -------------------------------------
# A performance is ONE continuous act at one venue - never phases, never a fresh
# deal partway through. The act carries three GOALS (first, second, final - each a
# taller number), a crowd whose patience DRAINS (the applause meter as a clock),
# and a hard DROP LIMIT. From the moment the FIRST goal falls you may CASH IN at
# any time - press the finish key, or simply catch every ball and hold them (the
# air empties, the act is over, the bow is implied) - and the results card pays
# what the whole act scored. Pushing for the next goal instead is the gamble: a
# COLLAPSE (crowd walks, drops, or empty stamina) strips the score back to the
# last goal actually reached. One optional CHALLENGE condition (the ENCORES deck)
# may colour the whole act - one condition for the whole act, never a different
# one per phase (Louis) - and the Backyard never rolls one, so the first stage
# stays a pure lesson in juggling.
const NUM_GOALS := 3
# the first goal per venue. The whole phase lives or dies on these numbers, so
# they were set against the --calib autoplayer sweep, not guessed: a flawless three-
# ball cascade clears the Backyard's first goal and falls well short of the Championship.
# The 7th entry (The Midnight Show, 2026-07-18) is a first guess extrapolating the
# same growth trend, NOT yet calib-verified like the rest - it is genuinely new,
# post-game content with no strong-player data behind it yet.
# A GOAL HAS TWO GATES: A NUMBER AND A CLOCK (2026-07-29, Louis: "make sure every single
# performance takes at least 15 seconds for the first reward and at least 60 seconds for the
# biggest reward, which can be improved marginally with tricks etc. (no 20 second biggest
# reward possible, and no 5 second first reward possible at all)").
#
# **A SCORE BAR ALONE CANNOT ENFORCE A TIME, and that is not a tuning problem, it is an
# arithmetic one.** The score is exponential now: a run carrying a x500 multiplier passes any
# fixed number in a handful of throws, so whatever the bar is set to, there exists a player who
# clears it in four seconds. The only thing that reliably enforces a minimum time is a minimum
# time. So a goal banks when BOTH gates open - and the two do different jobs:
#
#   * THE NUMBER is what makes a weak setup struggle. Three balls at the World Arena will not
#     reach the bar in sixty seconds however cleanly they are juggled.
#   * THE CLOCK is what stops a strong one finishing early. It is a floor, not a target.
#
# Tricks therefore move you from number-gated to clock-gated, which is a LARGE improvement if
# you were slow and a marginal one once you are already at the wall - exactly the shape Louis
# described. And the wall is absolute: no combination of props, counts, patterns and tricks can
# produce a five-second first goal or a twenty-second final one.
# THE FLOORS GROW WITH THE HOUSE (2026-07-29, Louis: "early stages such as backyard don't have
# to be that long: final score 30 seconds is enough"). A first act should be a minute of
# juggling, not two; the World Arena is where sixty seconds of holding a set is the ask. Both
# ends are his: 8/16/30 at the Backyard rising to 15/32/60 at the Midnight Show, interpolated
# across the ladder so no rung is a jump.
const GOAL_MIN_S_LOW := [8.0, 16.0, 30.0]    # ...at the Backyard
const GOAL_MIN_S_HIGH := [15.0, 32.0, 60.0]  # ...and at the last house
# THE ANTE, BALATRO-STYLE (Louis: "i want both exponential scoring and ante's. just like
# balatro"). It was `1 + 0.15 x ante` - linear, so the tenth act at a venue asked 2.5x the
# first, which against exponential scoring is no wall at all. It compounds now, which is what
# an ante IS: a venue you have played out stops being worth playing, and the game pushes you up
# the ladder rather than letting you farm the Backyard.
#
# CAPPED, though, and the cap is a safety rather than a nicety: reputation comes from goals, so
# a bar that grows without limit could wall a player out of the only venue they can reach and
# leave them unable to earn the reputation to leave it. Twelve is about six acts of growth.
# GENTLER THAN THE FIRST GUESS, because a rung is only ~6x and the ante has to fit UNDER that:
# at 1.45 capped at 12 the second act at a venue was already harder than the venue above it,
# which does not push a player up the ladder, it strands them. At 1.25 the ante reaches 2.4x
# after four acts and 6x after eight - a venue that quietly stops being worth farming.
const ANTE_GROWTH := 1.25
const ANTE_CAP := 6.0
